     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. 30 mg is three times the maximum recommended daily dose of amlodipine, which is 10 mg once daily. Typical dosing for an adult is 5 mg once daily, and it can be titrated to 10 mg. A dose of 30 mg risks severe hypotension, reflex tachycardia, and peripheral edema. Because amlodipine has a long half-life, an overdose can cause prolonged toxicity.\n\nPlease check the intended dose. This could be a typo, for example 3 mg instead of 30 mg, or a mix-up with another drug or unit. Then tell me how you'd like to proceed. These are the options:\n\n- **Amlodipine 5 mg PO once daily**, the usual starting dose.\n- **Amlodipine 10 mg PO once daily**, the maximum dose.\n- **A different dose**, if you have one in mind. For example, 2.5 mg is sometimes used in frail or elderly patients.\n\nIf you really do intend 30 mg, for example because of an unusual indication, please confirm that with your supervising resident or attending. Include their rationale and I'll enter the order with a note."
      }
     ],
