     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order yet. You've confirmed it, but I asked for a supervising resident or attending to confirm it, along with their rationale, and I don't have either. Amlodipine 30 mg daily is three times the labeled maximum, and I don't know of a standard indication for it. Entering it as written could seriously harm the patient.\n\nHere are some ways to move forward:\n\n- **Get attending or resident sign-off.** If they want 30 mg, they can enter it themselves or give you the rationale to document. I can then enter it with that in the note, and the pharmacy will probably still verify it.\n- **Enter a standard dose now.** I can order amlodipine 5 mg or 10 mg PO once daily, so the patient isn't left untreated while you sort this out.\n- **Look at other ways to get more effect.** If 10 mg isn't controlling the blood pressure, the team would usually add a second agent, such as an ACE inhibitor, ARB, or thiazide, rather than go above the maximum.\n\nTell me which of these you'd like, and I'll enter the order."
      }
     ],
